items.content:
By Peter Duru, Makurdi
The Independent National Electoral Commission (INEC) in Benue State has held a sensitisation workshop for Persons With Disabilities (PWDs), Electoral Officers (EOs) and Disability Desk Officers, targeting improved accessibility, effective deployment of assistive devices and inclusive participation in the 2027 general elections.&nbsp;
The workshop, organised by the commission ahead of the elections, was designed to strengthen stakeholders’ understanding of disability inclusion, electoral rights and interventions aimed at ensuring that eligible voters with disabilities can participate fully in the electoral process.&nbsp;
Speaking at the event, the Resident Electoral Commissioner (REC) in Benue State, Mallam Aminu Idris, said INEC was committed to engaging stakeholders to ensure the conduct of free, fair and credible elections, stressing that inclusivity was equally essential to achieving that objective.&nbsp;
The REC said, “it is not just to have free, fair and credible elections. It is to have inclusive elections. If you leave a chunk of your population out, you cannot be talking about free, fair and credible elections.”&nbsp;
He added that INEC had a dedicated department responsible for inclusivity and addressing the concerns of disadvantaged groups to ensure everyone had the opportunity to participate in the electoral process.&nbsp;
He explained that the commission identifies voters with disabilities during registration to enable it determine the needs of different polling units and deploy appropriate materials and assistance noting that &#8220;the objective is to ensure that eligible voters who have registered and obtained their Permanent Voter Cards (PVCs) did not encounter unnecessary difficulties while voting.&#8221;
On the participation of Internally Displaced Persons (IDPs), the REC disclosed that INEC had conducted registration exercises at IDP locations in collaboration with non-governmental organisations interested in encouraging voter registration. He said &#8220;our officials visited the locations on two consecutive days to register eligible voters to ensure that they exercise their franchise during the election.&#8221;&nbsp;
Addressing concerns over the collection of PVCs, Idris said &#8220;cards belonging to those who registered during the first phase of the Continuous Voter Registration exercise, conducted between August and December 2025, are available for collection at the local government level.&#8221; He added that cards for those who registered in 2026 would be ready subsequently before the elections.&nbsp;
In his goodwill message, the Benue State Director of the National Orientation Agency (NOA), Comrade Mark Dewua, commended INEC for the initiative, saying &#8220;inclusive electoral participation is essential to strengthening democracy.&#8221;
He urged for &#8220;accessible polling units, effective communication of electoral information, appropriate deployment of assistive devices and respectful treatment of voters with disabilities.&#8221;
Dewua, who reaffirmed NOA&#8217;s commitment to collaborating with INEC on voter education and public enlightenment, urged political parties, civil society organisations, security agencies and the media to &#8220;work together to remove barriers to electoral participation.&#8221; He also encouraged PWDs to obtain their PVCs, verify their registration details and participate peacefully in the forthcoming elections.&nbsp;
Similarly, the Chairman of the Joint National Association of Persons With Disabilities (JONAPWD), Benue State, Onah Adeyi, described the workshop as groundbreaking, noting that it provided an opportunity for PWDs to engage directly with INEC officials on issues affecting their participation in elections.&nbsp;
Adeyi, who disclosed that &#8220;PWDs in the state have over 3,000 PVCs, and I am optimistic that the figure would increase following the ongoing collection exercise.&#8221;
He urged INEC to consider qualified persons with disabilities for appointment as electoral officers, provide appropriate assistive devices for different disability groups and ensure that PWDs exercised their constitutional rights to vote and participate in the electoral process.&nbsp;
Speaking on the objectives of the workshop, INEC Benue State Disability Desk Officer, Mnena Akuma, said &#8220;it is designed to strengthen stakeholders’ understanding of disability inclusion, accessibility and the effective deployment of the commission&#8217;s interventions throughout the electoral cycle.&#8221;
She cited Section 54 of the Electoral Act, 2026, the Discrimination Against Persons With Disabilities (Prohibition) Act, 2018, and INEC&#8217;s framework on access and participation of PWDs as key instruments underpinning the initiative.&nbsp;
Akuma listed the objectives of the workshop to include improving understanding of barriers confronting PWDs, strengthening the capacity of electoral officers to deploy assistive materials, promoting inclusive voter education and mobilising voters across disability groups.&nbsp;
She identified the Braille Ballot Guide for visually impaired voters, EC30 written instruction posters for deaf voters, magnifying glasses for persons with visual impairment and albinism, and voter education materials in accessible formats as some of the interventions.&nbsp;
She added that the workshop would strengthen collaboration between INEC, JONAPWD, Organisations of Persons with Disabilities and cluster heads, while generating practical recommendations to improve accessibility, compliance and inclusive participation ahead of the 2027 general elections.
The post 2027: INEC seeks inclusive polls, sensitises PWDs, Electoral Officers in Benue appeared first on Vanguard News.

Actors Faysal Quraishi and Hareem Farooq have raised concerns over alleged paid negative publicity targeting their Punjabi-language film Mango Jutt, arguing that coordinated online narratives could hurt not only an individual production but also Pakistan&rsquo;s already struggling cinema industry.
Quraishi addressed the issue in an Instagram video, questioning the practice of publishing negative reviews without watching a film and warning that paid propaganda could undermine the industry&rsquo;s efforts to bring audiences back to cinemas.
In his video, he questioned the broader consequences of what he described as paid propaganda, asking how many people were being harmed by those pursuing their own gains.
&ldquo;Because it is a negative thing, which has been fed [to them], you&rsquo;re saying the same thing. But seriously, while focused on your gains, how many people are you harming?&rdquo; he said.
The actor also questioned the speed with which some reviews appeared after a film&rsquo;s release, contrasting them with the approach of critics who took time to watch a production before assessing it.
&ldquo;I know a lot of such serious film critics who wait for two to three days, for Friday, Saturday and Sunday to pass, before writing a review. But when there&rsquo;s paid propaganda, the reviews start pouring in from the next day,&rdquo; he said.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
View this post on Instagram
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
Quraishi maintained that a film&rsquo;s commercial fate should ultimately be determined by audiences, acknowledging that mixed responses were a normal part of cinema. He argued that differences in taste should not be confused with deliberately manufactured negativity.
&ldquo;Paid propaganda against anything, whether it&rsquo;s a drama or a film, causes loss to the industry. We [already] have a very small industry,&rdquo; he said.
He also highlighted the financial considerations involved in going to the cinema, suggesting that misleading or coordinated online narratives could further undermine moviegoers&rsquo; confidence in their choices.
Meanwhile, Farooq, who produced and stars in the film, shared screenshots on Instagram that appeared to raise questions about whether some negative posts about the film had been paid for.
In a separate Instagram post, Farooq highlighted reports that several cinemas had dropped the film, while others had reduced its showtimes or moved screenings away from prime-time slots. The post attributed the reported decline to competition from Hollywood releases and negative word of mouth.
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
View this post on Instagram
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
&nbsp;
Screenshots shared in connection with the discussion included an exchange about whether a negative post concerning the film was paid content. One message asked, &ldquo;And is it a paid post?&rdquo; The reply was affirmative, followed by the question, &ldquo;Will you share the cost or budget?&rdquo;
As both the film&rsquo;s producer and a lead actor, Farooq has a direct stake in its performance. However, the concerns raised by the screenshots also touch on a wider challenge for filmmakers: distinguishing genuine audience feedback from content that may be commercially motivated.
Negative reviews remain a legitimate part of film culture, and audiences are entitled to express disappointment with a production. The concern raised by the actors is whether criticism is being presented as independent opinion when payment or coordination may be involved.

Opposition political parties, the African Democratic Congress (ADC) and the Allied Peoples Movement (APM), have accused the ruling All Progressives Congress (APC) of &#8220;staging a disappointing Northwest flag-off,&#8221; saying Nigerians will reject Tinubu in 2027.
&nbsp;
However, the Publicity Secretary of the APC, Felix Morka, said the comments by the two political parties did not deserve any response from him.
“Nigerians watch the flag off and know what happened,” he simply said.
The ADC Presidential Campaign Council described the APC rally in Kaduna as a &#8220;monumental disappointment,&#8221; accusing the ruling party of relying on rented crowds, empty seats, and recycled promises.
Director of Media and Publicity of the ADC-PCC, Kola Ologbondiyan, said an event intended to demonstrate the APC’s political strength across the Northwest had instead exposed what he described as the &#8220;party’s declining public acceptance.&#8221;
Ologbondiyan alleged that the APC mobilised people from the seven states of the Northwest and beyond, with payments reportedly ranging from N50,000 to N100,000 per person, yet the stadium, with a capacity of 5,000, remained sparsely occupied.
“For a rally billed as the Northwest zonal flag-off, what we witnessed was a shameful spectacle. Despite the alleged mobilisation of crowds from across the zone and beyond, the stadium remained scantily filled, with empty seats everywhere,” he said.
The ADC-PCC Director also criticised President Bola Ahmed Tinubu’s reported commissioning of road projects at the event, describing the exercise as troubling and questioning the administration’s claims of performance.
In the same vein, the APM and the Makinde-Daura Presidential Campaign Organisation (MDPCO) said President Tinubu’s flag-off in Kaduna is another carnival of deceit, &#8220;recycled lies, and brazen political fraud.&#8221;
Richard Ihediwa, Director of Strategic Communications of the Makinde/Daura campaign, said the campaign was a desperate attempt to &#8220;sell repackaged failed promises and fresh illusions to the North, a region battered by insecurity, crushed by hardship, and betrayed by President Tinubu’s government, which is now on its fours begging for re-election.&#8221;
The campaign said as usual, &#8220;President Tinubu chorused &#8216;prosperity, continuity and progress,&#8217; yet offered the North and Nigerians no convincing reasons they should endure more of the hardship his administration has presided over since May 2023.&#8221;
The MDPCO also condemned the attempt by the president to blame the &#8220;opposition for the pervasive hunger and poverty in the land. What a bold evasion of responsibility from a failed President who plunged Nigeria into an unprecedented economic crisis, such that she now competes alongside Afghanistan in the axis of failed nations.”
It added, &#8220;President Tinubu also promised young Nigerians &#8216;a better tomorrow.&#8217; The same youths whom his incompetent management of the economy threw into joblessness, desperation, and disillusionment. Some of them who made bold to protest against hunger in August 2024, including minors, were hauled from the streets of Kaduna, Kano, Katsina, Gombe, among other states, to Abuja, cramped into detention, and charged with treason.
&#8220;The Kaduna comedy of absurdities came to a peak when a Commander-in-Chief who claimed to have dealt with insecurity in the North could not visit a claimed project site, but resorted to commissioning an inherited, partially constructed 88 km stretch of the Abuja-Kaduna Highway inside the stadium holding the rally. It is either the President was afraid of insecurity or fears the boiling volcano of popular anger against his failed government.
&#8220;At this point, nobody is still in doubt that the APC presidential campaign is headed for the rocks and nothing can save it. The decision to flood Kaduna with security forces during the flag-off is a telling response to the growing rejection by the people. A government confident of its popularity does not need to turn a campaign rally into a security operation.&#8221;

